\description{
A data frame containing comprehensive metadata for all epigenomes in the
Roadmap Epigenomics Project. This dataset includes information about
epigenome identifiers, standardized names, tissue types, biological groups,
quality classifications, and experimental details for 127 human reference
epigenomes.
}
\details{
This dataset contains metadata for all 127 reference epigenomes from the
Roadmap Epigenomics Project. This is the source metadata that has been
merged into \code{\link{roadmap_bed_gz_links_metadata}}, which already
contains the complete metadata merged with download URLs for DHS_promoter,
DHS_enhancer, and ChromHMM_15_states_5_coreMarks data types.

The \code{roadmap_metadata} dataset is useful for:
\itemize{
  \item Direct access to metadata without download URLs (when URLs are not
        needed)
  \item Understanding what metadata columns are available for filtering and
        analysis
  \item Filtering epigenomes by tissue type (\code{anatomy} column)
  \item Filtering epigenomes by biological group (\code{group} column)
  \item Accessing standardized names for display and reporting
  \item Assessing data quality using \code{class} and \code{quality_rating}
  \item Identifying available experimental data (e.g., H3K27ac via
        \code{has_k27ac})
}

For most use cases involving annotation with Roadmap data, you should use
\code{\link{roadmap_bed_gz_links_metadata}} instead, which contains this
metadata already merged with download URLs for each data type.

\strong{Key Columns for Common Use Cases:}
\itemize{
  \item \code{epigenome_id_eid}: Use this to identify specific epigenomes
        (e.g., in \code{EIDs} parameter of \code{\link{annotatePeaksWithRoadmap}})
  \item \code{anatomy}: Use this to filter by tissue type (e.g., "BRAIN",
        "BLOOD", "MUSCLE"). Note: values are in uppercase.
  \item \code{standardized_epigenome_name}: Use this for human-readable
        display names
  \item \code{class}: Use this to filter by data quality (prefer "Class 1"
        for highest quality)
}
}
\examples{

# Load the dataset
data(roadmap_metadata)

# Inspect the structure
head(roadmap_metadata)
dim(roadmap_metadata)

# Get all unique tissue types (anatomy)
unique_tissues <- unique(roadmap_metadata$anatomy)
unique_tissues <- unique_tissues[!is.na(unique_tissues)]
cat("Number of unique tissue types:", length(unique_tissues), "\n")
cat("Tissue types:", paste(sort(unique_tissues), collapse = ", "), "\n")

# Find all epigenomes for a specific tissue (e.g., "BRAIN")
brain_epigenomes <- roadmap_metadata[
    roadmap_metadata$anatomy == "BRAIN",
]
cat("Number of brain epigenomes:", nrow(brain_epigenomes), "\n")
print(brain_epigenomes[, c("epigenome_id_eid", "standardized_epigenome_name", "anatomy")])

# Find all Class 1 (highest quality) epigenomes
class1_epigenomes <- roadmap_metadata[
    roadmap_metadata$class == "Class 1",
]
cat("Number of Class 1 epigenomes:", nrow(class1_epigenomes), "\n")

# Find epigenomes with H3K27ac data available
k27ac_epigenomes <- roadmap_metadata[
    roadmap_metadata$has_k27ac == 1,
]
cat("Number of epigenomes with H3K27ac data:", nrow(k27ac_epigenomes), "\n")

# Get metadata for a specific EID
eid <- "E001"
eid_metadata <- roadmap_metadata[
    roadmap_metadata$epigenome_id_eid == eid,
]
if (nrow(eid_metadata) > 0) {
    cat("Epigenome:", eid_metadata$standardized_epigenome_name, "\n")
    cat("Tissue:", eid_metadata$anatomy, "\n")
    cat("Group:", eid_metadata$group, "\n")
    cat("Class:", eid_metadata$class, "\n")
} else {
    cat("EID", eid, "not found\n")
}

# Count epigenomes by group
group_counts <- table(roadmap_metadata$group)
cat("Epigenomes by group:\n")
print(sort(group_counts, decreasing = TRUE))

}
